WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.800
Welcome back, aspiring professional&nbsp;
Python devs to mCoding, I'm James Murphy.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.040
Let's learn how to set up logging in&nbsp;
your python application, the modern way.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:15.640
That means we're going to learn to stop&nbsp;
doing this, and start doing this instead.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:19.160
As always with so-called "best&nbsp;
practices" these are general guidelines

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.000
that work for most use cases.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.320
However, you know what's best for&nbsp;
your code way better than I do,

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.600
so if you have a good reason,&nbsp;
then do what you know is right.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.960
Don't blindly follow this advice&nbsp;
if it doesn't work for you.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.600
With that said, let's get going&nbsp;
... right after I sponsor myself.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.200
At mCoding, we do code reviews,&nbsp;
consulting, and professional training.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.560
Do you have a problem with your code?

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.000
Worried about your architecture?

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.240
Or do you just need some extra dev hours?

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.680
Why not make me part of your team?

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.960
Anyway let's get back to logging.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:53.200
The built-in logging package is very old&nbsp;
and full of stuff that you shouldn't use.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.760
It doesn't follow PEP8, the docs are a mess,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.560
and many tutorials are outdated or quite frankly&nbsp;
written by people who obviously don't use it.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.920
But for all its quirks, the&nbsp;
built-in logging package is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.520
the de-facto standard for logging in Python.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:10.160
It doesn't matter if you're using a cloud&nbsp;
provider or just developing a local application,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.800
the standard is the built-in logging package.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.400
But do you really even need logging at all?

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.600
Hey man, you came to me.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.960
If your app doesn't need logging, that's up to&nbsp;
you, I'm just here to show you what's possible.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.520
All right, let's see how to get started.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.640
You might think you just import logging,&nbsp;
make a logger, set it up with a basic config,

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.920
and then get to logging with your messages.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:36.160
When you run the code, based off the log level&nbsp;
you set, you see those level messages and worse.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.200
And I wouldn't blame you if&nbsp;
you've been doing it this way.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:45.240
In the most basic cases this is totally fine,&nbsp;
but I don't really recommend doing it this way.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.440
Why? Well, if you care to do logging&nbsp;
at all you probably want to log

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:53.280
to at least two different places,&nbsp;
like to stdout and also to a file.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.680
Or you want to log higher priority things&nbsp;
like errors and exceptions differently,

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.840
maybe you want to send an email if&nbsp;
there's an exception thrown in prod?

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.400
You can manually make handlers and&nbsp;
filters and add them to your loggers,

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.840
but, trust me, just don't do it that way.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.480
Instead, use dictConfig.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:16.120
For some reason dictConfig is hidden&nbsp;
away in this logging.config submodule,

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:20.160
and not so much as mentioned&nbsp;
in the main logging docs.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.520
As the name suggests, this lets you&nbsp;
configure logging via a dictionary

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.440
that explicitly lists all of the necessary&nbsp;
components of your logging setup,

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.240
namely the filters, formatters,&nbsp;
handlers, and loggers.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.720
The basic config hides these objects&nbsp;
and their relationships from you,

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:39.000
which makes for a really slick&nbsp;
one-liner in the most basic case,

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:44.680
but for more useful logging setups I find&nbsp;
it's much clearer to list them explicitly.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:46.160
It can get really confusing if you don't have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.640
good mental model for what&nbsp;
these things actually do.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:53.360
So here's the one picture to keep in&nbsp;
mind to see how it all fits together.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:56.400
Loggers are the things that&nbsp;
you actually use in your code.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.960
They have a ".info" and you can&nbsp;
call logger.info to log a message,

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:02.160
logger.debug to log a debug message,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.600
logger.exception to log the&nbsp;
in-flight exception, etc.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.080
That creates a log record,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.280
which is an object that contains all&nbsp;
kinds of useful contextual information.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.760
Things like the message and&nbsp;
severity, the current time,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:20.080
the current thread or async task, the&nbsp;
location in the source code, and so on.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:24.080
Every logger can set a level to drop&nbsp;
all messages below a certain severity,

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:28.480
and optionally some filters to drop&nbsp;
or alter messages along the way.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.760
So you could do something like drop all&nbsp;
messages that begin with "some annoying string."

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.840
Or you could do something more sophisticated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.400
like sensor private user data that&nbsp;
isn't supposed to appear in logs.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:45.280
The logger then passes these log records&nbsp;
off to each of their handlers one at a time.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.840
Handlers tell you how and where&nbsp;
to log a record, like to stdout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:53.520
to a file. over email. or&nbsp;
to a commercial log service.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.080
Each handler receives the log record and,&nbsp;
just like for loggers, the handler has a level

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.840
and some filters that allow it to drop&nbsp;
or alter the message along the way.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:06.840
if a record is dropped by a handler, it&nbsp;
still passes on to the rest of the handlers.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:12.080
But if it was dropped by the logger&nbsp;
itself, then it's dropped for good.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:14.600
Assuming the message passes&nbsp;
through the levels and filters,

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:19.520
when it comes time for the handler to actually&nbsp;
write the log message, it needs to write text.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.760
But currently this is a Python object.&nbsp;
So each handler has a formatter

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.760
that it uses to convert the log record&nbsp;
object into a string to be sent out.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:32.800
The formatter is what lets you customize&nbsp;
what an individual message looks like.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.720
Like is it the log level and then the message?&nbsp;
Or level, then timestamp, then message?

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:41.400
Or maybe you write JSON, or maybe&nbsp;
you write some other format.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.040
Formatters are typically where&nbsp;
you see the most customization

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:46.640
because it's the formatter&nbsp;
that selects which data from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:49.040
the log record to actually include in the message

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:53.360
and that depends a lot on your specific use&nbsp;
case and what you want to see in your logs.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:56.120
And this is almost the complete picture.

00:04:56.120 --> 00:05:06.400
Except this is the picture for the root logger,&nbsp;
the root as in the root of the tree of loggers.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.840
Loggers are accessed and created by name,

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:13.800
and if you split the name by dots then&nbsp;
you end up with a tree of loggers.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:18.840
So the A.X logger is a child of the A&nbsp;
logger, which is a child of the root.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:23.720
By default, once a child's done handling a log&nbsp;
record, it passes that record up to its parent.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:27.880
So if this log record was generated&nbsp;
down here in the A.X logger,

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:32.080
all of A.X's handlers would run,&nbsp;
then it would propagate up to A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:34.320
and all of A's handlers would run,

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:38.520
then it would propagate up to the root&nbsp;
and all of the roots handlers would run.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.480
This is done to make it easier for users&nbsp;
to disable messages from whole subsystems

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.120
just by disabling certain loggers.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:48.760
Once again, if a record is dropped by&nbsp;
a handler it will continue moving on,

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:51.200
to include propagating up to the parent.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.440
But if it's dropped by a logger,&nbsp;
then it stops and doesn't propagate.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:01.840
But slow down there, this is way more&nbsp;
flexibility than you would usually need.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:05.080
Having all these handlers and filters&nbsp;
and propagation at different levels

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:08.680
is unnecessarily complex for most use cases.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:11.720
And it often leads to subtly broken configs.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:13.080
So here's what I recommend.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:17.040
See all these non-root handlers? Delete them.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.600
Unless you've got a good reason,&nbsp;
put all handlers on the root logger.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.200
This is simpler, but also having all&nbsp;
handlers on the root logger ensures

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.120
that any messages generated by third-party&nbsp;
libraries get logged and formatted

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:33.280
the same way as messages&nbsp;
generated by your own application.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:35.720
Filters? Same deal.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:38.440
There's a decent chance you&nbsp;
don't need any filters at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:40.840
but if you do put them all on the root logger

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:43.040
or their handlers.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.240
Leave propagation turned on, which is the default,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:49.880
so that all messages propagate&nbsp;
up to the root logger.

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:55.000
However, don't use the root logger in your code.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:00.400
If you use any of the top-level logging functions&nbsp;
like logging.info, this uses is the root logger.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.320
So don't use any of those functions.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.280
Make sure to use your own logger, which you get by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:10.680
using logging.getLogger and passing in&nbsp;
the name of the logger that you want.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:13.840
This will create the logger first&nbsp;
if it doesn't already exist,

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.840
then you can use your logger.info&nbsp;
instead of logging.info.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.720
And remember, your logger&nbsp;
doesn't have any handlers on it.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:24.200
We're depending on propagation to&nbsp;
send all events up to the root logger

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:27.520
and have the root logger&nbsp;
actually handle the events.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:31.880
If you have a small- to medium-sized application,&nbsp;
a single non-root logger is all you need.

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:33.680
If you have a very large application,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:39.320
then you should make one non-root logger for&nbsp;
each major subcomponent of your application.

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:41.400
You definitely don't need a logger for every file.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:46.000
That'd be a waste because these are globals&nbsp;
that live for the entire life of the program.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.200
With that all in mind, let's get back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:51.600
to configuring logging for a few&nbsp;
common setups using dictConfig.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:55.960
As a baseline let's just have a simple&nbsp;
config that logs everything to stdout.

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:57.760
If you're ever confused about the config,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:02.080
draw it out like this and then use&nbsp;
the picture to fill in the config.

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:05.240
This "version" is required&nbsp;
and the only valid value is 1.

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:08.840
This is so that they can change everything&nbsp;
in the future without breaking old code.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:10.840
"disable_existing_loggers" does what it says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:14.560
it disables anything that's not&nbsp;
explicitly listed in this config.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:20.480
I'm going to go ahead and set this to false so&nbsp;
that I can get log messages from third-party code.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:25.120
No filters in this configuration,&nbsp;
so let's just delete that.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:29.400
Next define a formatter named "simple"&nbsp;
and give it a simple format string.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:31.480
We didn't specify what class this formatter is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:35.520
so by default it just uses the&nbsp;
built-in logging.Formatter.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:40.600
It accepts the format string like this, using&nbsp;
this kind of weird printf-style format string.

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:44.640
Yes it's kind of weird but just deal with it or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:49.440
foreshadowing, wait a minute&nbsp;
and we'll see a better way.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:51.920
If you want to customize your own&nbsp;
format you can find a list of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:55.800
the available variables in&nbsp;
the logging documentation.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:02.520
Next we need to define the single stdout handler,&nbsp;
so we create this single handler named "stdout"

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.760
and set our "simple" formatter as&nbsp;
the formatter for this handler.

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:10.280
In order to get it to actually&nbsp;
log to stdout, we set its class to

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:14.840
the built-in logging stream handler&nbsp;
with a stream of sys.stdout.

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:18.080
The "ext://" slash here means "external",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:22.480
as in this is a variable that's&nbsp;
defined outside of this config.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:29.200
And voila! In just 16 lines we've configured&nbsp;
what the basic config did in one line.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:31.760
I know, I know, but keep in mind this more of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:35.120
verbose style is going to be a lot&nbsp;
clearer when we have more going on.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:37.280
So stick with me.

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:39.360
Despite the fact that we're using dictConfig,

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:42.320
that doesn't mean that we need to&nbsp;
keep the logging configuration as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:45.360
a literal dictionary in our Python source.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:47.440
Nothing wrong with that,&nbsp;
but many find it convenient

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:52.280
to keep the logging config in a&nbsp;
separate file in JSON or YAML format.

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:57.920
Personally I prefer to keep my config in JSON, so&nbsp;
go ahead and create a JSON version of your config,

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:01.400
then load that JSON when&nbsp;
when your application starts.

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:02.720
If you wanted to use YAML instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:06.120
it would look basically the same except&nbsp;
of course you would have a YAML config

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:10.800
and you would "import yaml" and do&nbsp;
a YAML load instead of a JSON load.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:14.560
Let's bring those side by side just&nbsp;
so you can see the two configs.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:19.520
Obviously YAML is a lot more condensed, but I&nbsp;
find it to be a lot more error-prone as well.

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:22.800
And also there's no YAML parser built into Python,

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:25.800
whereas there is a built-in JSON parser.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:28.720
So if you wanted to, you would have&nbsp;
to pull that in as a dependency.

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:31.040
"pyyaml" is a popular choice.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:34.600
Keeping the log config in a separate&nbsp;
file also allows you to let your users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:36.680
adjust the logging config to their preference.

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:44.360
You know, if you trust your&nbsp;
users to do that kind of thing.

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:48.040
Second setup. Let's modify the&nbsp;
config so that errors go to stderr,

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:51.840
and then all logs go to a file.

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:57.240
Change the "stdout" handler to "stderr"&nbsp;
and set its level to "WARNING".

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:01.800
Then, create a new handler and set&nbsp;
its class to a RotatingFileHandler.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:05.920
A rotating file handler keeps appending logs&nbsp;
to a file until it reaches a certain size,

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:08.360
in this case 10 kilobytes.

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:12.440
After it reaches 10 kilobytes, it&nbsp;
creates a backup and starts a new file.

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:16.320
After three backups it starts&nbsp;
deleting the oldest one.

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:20.360
10 kilobytes is a pretty small limit, this is&nbsp;
just so that you can see the rollover happen.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:28.960
You probably want to pick a few megabytes.

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:38.600
After running the script a bunch of&nbsp;
times, you can see it eventually created

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:43.440
this "my_app.log.1" and then&nbsp;
started using "my_app.log" again.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:45.240
We're still using the "simple" formatter here.

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:49.400
But since we're saving to a log file,&nbsp;
why don't we include some extra details?

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:51.400
We accomplish that by adding this new "detailed"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:55.200
formatter and setting it as the&nbsp;
formatter for the "file" handler.

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:56.960
We include much more information in the format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:59.680
string, and we're also showing&nbsp;
off the "datefmt" format here,

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:02.320
which allows us to customize&nbsp;
how dates are printed.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:09.400
Pro tip: use an ISO-8601-compliant format&nbsp;
and include the timezone. Trust me.

00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:13.240
This way our log contains a lot&nbsp;
more useful contextual information.

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:17.040
For a lot of applications,&nbsp;
this is a great place to stop.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:21.240
But, if you really care about&nbsp;
the quality of your log data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:25.040
then I really suggest making one crucial change.

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:26.760
Take a look at this log file.

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:31.040
Glancing over it I can visually distinguish&nbsp;
different messages from each other,

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:34.200
but notice that we have tracebacks in here.

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:37.400
And what if log messages had newlines in them?

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:40.960
If I wanted to parse this programmatically&nbsp;
I'd need to be able to parse back out

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:42.920
all of the data that I put into it.

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:47.440
But it's kind of just in free-form text with&nbsp;
newlines that could be anywhere at this point.

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:50.240
That's kind of intractable.

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:54.000
The solution? Store persistent&nbsp;
logs in JSON format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.920
so that they can be parsed easily later on.

00:12:56.920 --> 00:13:00.040
This is a change in how to convert&nbsp;
a log record into a string,

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:04.840
so that's the job of the formatter,&nbsp;
we need a JSON formatter.

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:10.520
But oh wait, there's no&nbsp;
built-in logging JSON formatter.

00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:13.840
There are a few you can pip install,&nbsp;
but let's just write our own.

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:17.320
Supposing we did, you'd think you'd&nbsp;
be able to just set the "class" key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:20.240
here and then pass in whatever arguments here,

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:23.240
and those will be keyword&nbsp;
arguments to the constructor.

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:26.440
That's what's happening here&nbsp;
with the handler, right?

00:13:26.440 --> 00:13:27.320
Uhh, nope.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:31.040
You can use your own class&nbsp;
here using the "class" keyword,

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:38.680
but if you do then all of the keys are&nbsp;
hardcoded to be the ones that the built-in uses/

00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:44.040
So I could use "format" and "datefmt",&nbsp;
but I couldn't create my own "fmt_keys".

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:48.360
Why is it like this way?&nbsp;
Great question! Moving on.

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:53.200
Change "class" to "()" and then&nbsp;
it will do what you actually want:

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:57.000
call this and pass this as a keyword argument.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:59.240
You'd have to do the same thing&nbsp;
anywhere else in the config,

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:05.040
like if I made my own custom handler that didn't&nbsp;
have the same interface as the built-in one.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:08.200
Okay well with that weird road bump&nbsp;
out of the way, let's continue.

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:10.240
So we're going to pass in these format keys,

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:17.400
which is going to be a dictionary where the key is&nbsp;
the key that I want to appear in the log message,

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:19.640
and the value here, like "levelname",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:24.120
is the variable that we're going&nbsp;
to look up from the log record.

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:27.120
Okay so let's go ahead and write this class.

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:31.840
We're in a new file here and we just&nbsp;
inherit from the built-in loging Formatter.

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:36.640
Nothing special in the init, we just store&nbsp;
the format keys that we get from the config.

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:38.680
Then we need to define this "format" function.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:41.800
This is a thing that takes the&nbsp;
record and goes to a string.

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:45.480
I'm using "@override" here to indicate that&nbsp;
we're overriding something from the parent class.

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:49.440
It's not strictly necessary, but it's a&nbsp;
good habit to get into marking these things.

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:51.720
All we do is extract out the record data into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:56.120
dictionary and then use the "json"&nbsp;
module to dump that to a string.

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:58.680
As far as actually extracting those&nbsp;
fields goes, it's pretty simple.

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:01.960
Regardless of the config, I chose to include the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:07.200
message and a timestamp in&nbsp;
ISO format in UTC timezone.

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:10.680
We pull in any exception data if&nbsp;
it's present using some parent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:13.240
methods in order to extract things out nicely.

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:17.080
And for the rest of the keys we just grab&nbsp;
them from the attributes of the record.

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:21.040
It's pretty straightforward and you&nbsp;
could probably do whatever you want here.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:28.000
And donzo! Update the config to use the&nbsp;
new JSON formatter and we're good to go.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:32.120
Check our log file and we&nbsp;
see nicely formatted JSON.

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:35.320
A slight warning though,&nbsp;
this file is not valid JSON.

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:37.920
Each line is valid JSON.

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:41.840
This format is called JSON Lines and&nbsp;
the common file extension is ".jsonl".

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:47.640
So to parse it you just read the file&nbsp;
line by line and parse each line as JSON.

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:50.400
And double pro-tip: now&nbsp;
that we're outputting JSON,

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:54.480
it's actually really easy to add&nbsp;
lots of extra contextual information.

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:58.600
To do this we can just use the "extra"&nbsp;
argument in one of our log calls.

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:02.920
Give it a dictionary of extra information and&nbsp;
Python will stuff that onto the log record.

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:04.880
Then just update your formatter to pull in those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:10.320
extra attributes and now any&nbsp;
extras will appear in our JSON.

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:13.640
Here's the {"x": "hello"}.

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:17.640
If you're getting too many logs and you'd&nbsp;
like finer control over which ones to drop,

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:20.040
then you might need a filter.

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:24.600
The process of creating a custom filter is&nbsp;
very similar to creating a custom formatter.

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:28.520
Inherit from the built-in Filter, then define&nbsp;
your own override of the "filter" function.

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:33.720
Given a record, you return a bool to indicate&nbsp;
whether or not that record should be processed.

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:38.240
So this non-error filter does kind of the&nbsp;
opposite of setting the level to "INFO".

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:41.800
Setting the level to "INFO" would mean&nbsp;
that you would only keep messages that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:45.160
were "INFO", "WARNING", "ERROR", "CRITICAL",

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:49.680
but setting this non-error filter would&nbsp;
give you "DEBUG" and "INFO" instead.

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:51.360
You can also alter the record here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:55.000
like if you wanted to censor private&nbsp;
data or return an altered copy.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:58.960
I'm not going to deal with filters for the rest&nbsp;
of the video, but here's a homework exercise.

00:16:58.960 --> 00:17:05.360
Using this non-error filter, create a logging&nbsp;
config that shows normal messages to stdout,

00:17:05.360 --> 00:17:07.240
but error messages to stderr.

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:11.080
You're going to need the&nbsp;
filter to prevent duplicates.

00:17:11.080 --> 00:17:17.320
Okay okay, surely there are no more glaring&nbsp;
flaws with this logging setup, right?

00:17:17.320 --> 00:17:22.800
At the risk of using a forbidden word&nbsp;
in Python let's talk about performance.

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:26.440
By its very nature, calling a&nbsp;
log function results in I/O.

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:31.200
If a user makes a request to my web app&nbsp;
and that results in 10 log messages,

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:37.360
I don't want to add 10 round-trips worth of time&nbsp;
to my logging service before I respond to my user.

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:42.280
But currently that's what will happen because&nbsp;
all logging calls are synchronous and blocking.

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:47.800
The solution? Use a QueueHandler&nbsp;
to log off the main thread.

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:49.720
Collecting log data isn't the slow part.

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:53.120
The slow part is sending&nbsp;
it wherever it needs to go.

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:56.960
A queue handler stores your log&nbsp;
records in a queue without blocking,

00:17:56.960 --> 00:18:00.720
while an associated queue&nbsp;
listener accepts those messages

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:04.640
and passes them off to other&nbsp;
handlers in another thread.

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:08.560
In order to configure this, create&nbsp;
a new queue handler in your config.

00:18:08.560 --> 00:18:13.960
The class is "logging.handlers.QueueHandler"&nbsp;
and then it accepts another list of handlers.

00:18:13.960 --> 00:18:15.880
These are the handlers that it dispatches to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:19.680
so basically take the handlers that&nbsp;
you had on the root handler before,

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:26.840
put them here and then change the queue&nbsp;
handler to be your only handler on the root.

00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:30.600
This "respect_handler_level" for&nbsp;
for some reason is by default false,

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:36.360
which results in the behavior of sending every&nbsp;
message to every handler regardless of log level,

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:38.680
so yeah, that's probably not what we want.

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:42.520
I'm going to set this to true&nbsp;
so that it does what you expect.

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:45.160
There's one more thing we need to&nbsp;
handle over here in the main file,

00:18:45.160 --> 00:18:48.480
which is that because the queue&nbsp;
handler is starting a thread,

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:50.640
that's not something that's&nbsp;
going to happen automatically.

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:54.840
When we set up our logging we need&nbsp;
to manually start that thread.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:57.560
We accomplish this by getting the&nbsp;
queue handler by name and then

00:18:57.560 --> 00:19:01.160
if it exists we start its listener's thread.

00:19:01.160 --> 00:19:05.200
We also register an atexit callback in&nbsp;
order to call the listener's stop method

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:08.240
and shut it down gracefully when the program ends.

00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:10.920
Alternatively, if you want to keep&nbsp;
all the work inside the config,

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:16.280
you could also subclass the queue handler&nbsp;
class and make it do this stuff in its init.

00:19:16.280 --> 00:19:17.200
And success!

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:20.840
We finally have a high quality,&nbsp;
parsable, multi-destination,

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:24.800
non-blocking logging setup&nbsp;
for our Python application.

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:27.400
Feels good, doesn't it?

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:32.480
But notice I said logging for your&nbsp;
"application" not your "library".

00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:37.600
Application authors know who their users are and&nbsp;
know what kind of logs that they want to see.

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:39.920
Whereas if you're writing library code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:44.400
you don't know who your end user is and&nbsp;
you don't know what kind of logs they want.

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:49.320
Conclusion: for library code,&nbsp;
don't configure logging.

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:53.920
You can still use logging, create loggers,&nbsp;
log messages and other important events.

00:19:53.920 --> 00:19:58.280
Just don't configure it with&nbsp;
dictConfig or any other config.

00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:00.720
Let applications do the configuring.

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:04.920
If a user doesn't configure logging,&nbsp;
the default and expected behavior is

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:07.200
that warnings and above will be printed to stderr.

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:10.920
If a user of your library does configure logging,

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:13.640
then don't interfere with what&nbsp;
they want by adding handlers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:16.880
formatters or other things&nbsp;
that they're not aware of.

00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:18.160
Finally, do you remember log4j?

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:24.520
It's an extremely popular logging library for&nbsp;
Java that had a 0-day vulnerability wreak havoc

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:29.520
and cause absolute pandemonium in the business&nbsp;
world as thousands of large products and services

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:34.160
with millions or billions of users&nbsp;
instantly became vulnerable to an easy-to-do

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:37.720
remote arbitrary code execution vulnerability.

00:20:37.720 --> 00:20:41.800
At the heart of the vulnerability was&nbsp;
a combination of logging user input,

00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:46.680
combined with a plugin that allowed&nbsp;
loading remote data as Java object data.

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:51.120
Anyway here's Python's "makeLogRecord" function&nbsp;
that can be used to create log records manually.

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:56.560
For example "from a pickled&nbsp;
event received over the wire".

00:20:56.560 --> 00:20:59.000
I'm not saying this is actively vulnerable.

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:02.840
but in-case "logging4p"&nbsp;
becomes a thing ... called it.

